Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005 (@gc-mit112: 0, is #reference, #part-of @gc-sources) { r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG
Christopher Cummins and Sylvia Ceyer. Principles of Chemical Science, MIT 5.112, Fall 2005. MIT OpenCourseWare. [Course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-112-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2005/) · [Calendar](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-112-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2005/pages/calendar/) · [Readings](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-112-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2005/pages/readings/).

Use the complete lecture progression as a demanding second treatment after the first general-chemistry course. Its description expects unusually strong chemistry preparation and recommends single-variable calculus. Later topics include molecular orbitals, coordination, color/magnetism, ligand substitution, metals/semiconductors, and nuclear applications. Teach or follow missing mathematical preparation when a derivation uses it; do not place this course before the easy entrance.

The reading table refers to Atkins and Jones, Chemical Principles, 3rd edition, 2004, a separately acquired commercial textbook. The calendar mentions on-campus problem sets/exams, but the published OCW resource menu does not supply the same complete problem/exam collection as 5.111SC. Do not describe unavailable campus assessments as downloadable. OCW materials remain external under their own notices. Course identity, prerequisites, calendar, reading edition, and available resource types checked on 29 October 2026.

